ting $\\mathcal{P}$-positions. In the first version, there is a simple pattern. In the other two versions, we find partial solutions in each case through two experimental routes. First by limiting the number of piles, then by limiting the number of tokens per pile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaking and Breaking Games (https://dspace.cvut.cz/server/api/core/bitstreams/2307de3e-be01-4993-92f8-cccc588d8207/content)\nciteturn4search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; but the heaps up to 100, 000 exhibit a remarkable fractal-like set of nim-values ... ¨3X, 8 ď X ď E, ... Let A be a set of positive integers, called the cut num-\n3.\nTaking and Breaking Games\nS such that Gpn ` pq “ Gpnq ` s except all n such that\nn “ Ppq ` 1, mq ` k,\nfor all k P S, q, m ě 0,\nwhere Ppq, mq :“ rpq ¨ 2m`1 ` 2m ` 1q.\nFor these values we have\nG pPpq ` 1, mq ` kq “ G pPpq, mq ` kq ` 2m`3.\nAnother interesting game is ¨9 which has not been completely analyzed,\nbut the heaps up to 100, 000 exhibit a remarkable fractal-like set of nim-values\n[57, A3].\nWe end this section with a summary of unsolved two-digit hexadecimal\ngames.\nOpen Problem 8. [48, GONC5, A3] Resolve any of the following unsolved\nhexadecimal games:\n¨2X, A ď X ď F,\n¨1X, X P t8, 9, C, D, E, Fu,\n¨4X, X P t9, B, D, Fu,\n¨3X, 8 ď X ď E,\n¨6X, 8 ď X ď F,\n¨5X, 8 ď X ď F,\n¨9X, 1 ď X ď A,\n¨7X, 8 ď X ď F,\n¨BX, X P t6, 9, Du,\n¨9D,\n¨FX, X P t4, 6, 7u.\n¨DX, 1 ď X ď F,\n3.6\nPure Breaking Games\nIn view of the above results, we can notice there is a great gap between\nunderstanding octal and hexadecimal games. It appears that the complexity\nof the nim sequences has a tendency to increase while we increase the number\nof possible splits of a heap. In this section, we will try examine this tendency\nin more detail. This can be done by disallowing the removing tokens from a\nheap in order to examine these games in an isolation.\nThis class of so called pure breaking games was first described and analyzed\nin separation by Dailly et al. in 2018 [11]. They define these games as follows.\nDefinition 3.86. Let A be a set of positive integers, called the cut num-\nbers. We define a Pure Breaking Game PBpLq as the heap game with the\nfollowing options\nHn :“ tHa1 ` . . . ` Haku,\nsuch that ℓP L and a0 ` a1 ` . . . ` aℓ“ n with ai ą 0 for all i ě 0. In other\nwords, in PBpLq a player’s move consist of choosing a heap and splitting it\ninto ℓ` 1 non-empty heaps with ℓP L being his cut number. We will call this\nmove an ℓ-cut.\n82--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBanking | Thematic (https://cdn1.i3investor.com/my/files/dfgs88n/2019/11/06/1512394725--1949422048.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.0 years ago; Chart with blue bars for NIM (RHS), dark blue line for Loans growth (LHS), and red line for Deposits growth (LHS). ... 2015: Cut loan prime rate (LPR) 3x, reduced reserved requirement ratio (RRR) 4x, removed the floating deposit rate ceiling; WeBank launched in 1Q15 while MYbank introduced in 2Q15\nBanking | Thematic\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure #6: Key trends observed in China before and after the advent of digital banks\n\nChart with blue bars for NIM (RHS), dark blue line for Loans growth (LHS), and red line for Deposits growth (LHS). X-axis quarters: 1Q14, 3Q14, 1Q15, 3Q15, 1Q16, 3Q16, 1Q17, 3Q17, 1Q18, 3Q18, 1Q19. Left y-axis labeled yoy from 3% to 15%. Right y-axis from 1.5% to 2.9%.\n\nCallouts:\n- WeBank at 1Q15\n- MYbank at 2Q15\n- Sichuan XW Bank at 4Q16\n- Citic aiBank & ICBC Xiaobai at 4Q17\n\nNotes below chart:\n2015: Cut loan prime rate (LPR) 3x, reduced reserved requirement ratio (RRR) 4x, removed the floating deposit rate ceiling; WeBank launched in 1Q15 while MYbank introduced in 2Q15\n2016: Spillover from 2015, transition to VAT from biz tax (interest income reported on a net basis under VAT), Reduced RRR 1x; Sichuan XW Bank launched